{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2098 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5effb204b2438ab3e9b3db0c5742146205d6f8fd47b590dd9bbc2ccaca48dbb8" :edb/db-before-t 2097 :edb/db-after-t 2098 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535410 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:33:58.628Z" 13194139535410 true] [17592186119865 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347753 13194139535410 true] [17592186119865 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347767 13194139535410 true] [17592186119867 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347760 13194139535410 true] [17592186120034 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535410 true] [17592186120034 1100 "Given the curve $r(t) = ⟨2t^{2} - t, 5 - 3t^{2}⟩$, find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨ - 1, 2⟩$." 13194139535410 false] [17592186120034 1100 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{2}-t,5-3{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨-1,2⟩.$" 13194139535410 true] [17592186120034 1145 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t-1,-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨-1\\,,\\,2⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨-1,2⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t-1,-6t⟩ & =c⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t-1,-6t⟩ & =⟨-c,2c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4t-1=-c \\\\ -6t=2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=-4t+1.$ Substituting $c=-4t+1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6t & =2(-4t+1) \\\\ -6t & =-8t+2 \\\\ 2t & =2 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨-1,2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{2}-1,5-3(1)^{2}⟩=⟨1,2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,2).$" 13194139535410 true] [17592186120147 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535410 true] [17592186120147 1100 "Given the curve $r(t) = ⟨2t^{2}, e^{t}, 2e^{t}⟩$, find all of the points at which $r^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." 13194139535410 false] [17592186120147 1100 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{2}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." 13194139535410 true] [17592186120147 1145 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ & =c\\,⟨4t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ & =⟨4ct,\\,c{e}^{t},\\,2c{e}^{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}=4ct \\\\ {e}^{t}=c{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}=2c{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we obtain that $c=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} & =1\\cdot 4t \\\\ {t}^{2} & =2t \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t & =0 \\\\ t(t-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=0$ and $2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨2(0)^{2},\\,{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2(2)^{2},\\,{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(0,1,2)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2}).$" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347753 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347754 13194139535410 true] [17592186347753 1089 "q-126835" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347753 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535410 true] [17592186347753 1100 "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨3t+5,1-{t}^{3}⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347753 1113 #uuid "28b07468-67e6-5160-b480-58f04dacf3f5" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347753 1145 "At the point where $t=0,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨3(0)+5,1-{0}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨3,-3(0)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347755 13194139535410 true] [17592186347754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347756 13194139535410 true] [17592186347754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347757 13194139535410 true] [17592186347754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347758 13194139535410 true] [17592186347754 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347759 13194139535410 true] [17592186347754 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535410 true] [17592186347754 1041 "selection" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347754 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347756 13194139535410 true] [17592186347754 1110 #uuid "cfba4061-161c-5e71-9c8e-3ddfb8354902" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347755 1034 "⟨3,-1⟩+u⟨1,5⟩" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347755 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535410 true] [17592186347755 1111 #uuid "cbd9cf26-36a3-573f-be14-4292f29ef821" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347756 1034 "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347756 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535410 true] [17592186347756 1111 #uuid "19c9af6e-fd42-5fe8-a8a0-4ef5c76c9b79" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347757 1034 "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,-3⟩" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347757 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535410 true] [17592186347757 1111 #uuid "d79405e0-cf4f-56f0-ba10-aaf9cddf88c4" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347758 1034 "⟨3,-1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347758 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535410 true] [17592186347758 1111 #uuid "4d3f6f05-e2b9-5329-9364-d0192d174f99" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347759 1034 "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨1,5⟩" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347759 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535410 true] [17592186347759 1111 #uuid "0d66860a-50b2-5812-a611-399d148a0e71" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347760 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347761 13194139535410 true] [17592186347760 1089 "q-164840" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347760 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535410 true] [17592186347760 1100 "Given the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-2)\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." 13194139535410 true] [17592186347760 1113 #uuid "0d4944e3-e48d-5ad3-888c-09e6883cda6e" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347760 1145 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [({t}^{2}-2)\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j]\\cdot (2t\\,i+2t\\,j) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}-2)\\cdot 2t+{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t & =0 \\\\ 2t(2{t}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 4t({t}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 4t(t-1)(t+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =((1)^{2}-2)\\,i+(1)^{2}\\,j=-\\,i+j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(-1,1).$" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347761 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347762 13194139535410 true] [17592186347761 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347763 13194139535410 true] [17592186347761 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347764 13194139535410 true] [17592186347761 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347765 13194139535410 true] [17592186347761 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347766 13194139535410 true] [17592186347761 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535410 true] [17592186347761 1041 "selection" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347761 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347765 13194139535410 true] [17592186347761 1110 #uuid "43088988-e045-502f-b9da-394309ab53c5" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347762 1034 "$(2,4)$ and $(-1,1)$" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347762 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535410 true] [17592186347762 1111 #uuid "8e0ba96f-fca2-5b85-a4a8-c7ca577b4743" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347763 1034 "$(7,9)$ and $(14,16)$" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347763 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535410 true] [17592186347763 1111 #uuid "e2033a63-1ab3-5f1e-99af-a83fcad0e314" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347764 1034 "(2,4)" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347764 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535410 true] [17592186347764 1111 #uuid "27768cb2-e09c-5f1e-863e-f00fbca3eafe" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347765 1034 "(-1,1)" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347765 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535410 true] [17592186347765 1111 #uuid "2de6936d-188d-5b1b-92bb-57ef40e0405f" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347766 1034 "(14,16)" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347766 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535410 true] [17592186347766 1111 #uuid "cbe7a385-9e89-5968-927b-cfb29a8fb0d6" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347767 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347768 13194139535410 true] [17592186347767 1089 "q-75203" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347767 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535410 true] [17592186347767 1100 "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347767 1113 #uuid "f79a0634-b781-513c-bc7a-42650fc401f4" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347767 1145 "At the point where $t=0$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347768 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347769 13194139535410 true] [17592186347768 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347770 13194139535410 true] [17592186347768 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347771 13194139535410 true] [17592186347768 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347772 13194139535410 true] [17592186347768 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347773 13194139535410 true] [17592186347768 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535410 true] [17592186347768 1041 "selection" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347768 1104 #edb/ref 17592186347773 13194139535410 true] [17592186347768 1110 #uuid "288cbfdd-3be2-5693-8506-6a82d50d6f5b" 13194139535410 true] [17592186347769 1034 "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩